=== Complete Solutions

#sol-item(1, "C", dense: true)[
  Xylem, whose dead lignified vessels carry water and salts upwards from the root.
]

#sol-item(2, "C", dense: true)[
  Water moves from the more dilute soil solution into the more concentrated cell sap through a partially permeable membrane — osmosis.
]

#sol-item(3, "B", dense: true)[
  Dry air and wind keep the water potential gradient steep, so water is lost faster.
]

#sol-item(4, "B", dense: true)[
  They must be moved against the concentration gradient, which requires energy from respiration.
]

#sol-item(5, "B", dense: true)[
  Transpiration is evaporation of water from the leaf surfaces, mainly through the stomata.
]

#sol-item(6, "A", dense: true)[
  Phloem carries sucrose and amino acids, upwards and downwards.
]

#sol-item(7, "A", dense: true)[
  Wind removes the humid layer around the leaf, steepening the diffusion gradient.
]

#sol-item(8, "B", dense: true)[
  Active transport of mineral salts requires ATP from respiration.
]

#sol-item(9, "B", dense: true)[
  Evaporation from the leaves creates tension that pulls the water column upwards.
]

#sol-item(10, "C", dense: true)[
  Phloem is living tissue, its cells separated by sieve plates, carrying sugar and amino acids.
]

#sol-item(11, "D", dense: true)[
  The waxy layer is impermeable to water, so water cannot evaporate directly through the epidermis.
]

#sol-item(12, "C", dense: true)[
  Movement of water through a partially permeable membrane system is osmosis.
]

#sol-item(13, "A", dense: true)[
  An air lock in the xylem would stop water movement and ruin the measurement.
]

#sol-item(14, "B", dense: true)[
  Sunken stomata trap humid air and a thick cuticle resists evaporation — both reduce water loss.
]

#sol-item(15, "A", dense: true)[
  Water loss exceeds uptake, the cells become flaccid and the tissue can no longer support itself.
]

#sol-item(16, "D", dense: true)[
  Translocation is the transport of dissolved organic substances in the phloem.
]

#sol-item(17, "C", dense: true)[
  Liquid water exuded at the leaf margins through hydathodes when root pressure is high.
]

#sol-item(18, "C", dense: true)[
  Osmosis is passive; the other three are active processes.
]

#sol-item(19, "C", dense: true)[
  The bark contains the phloem, so the roots are starved of sugar.
]

#sol-item(20, "B", dense: true)[
  Transpiration pull, transmitted down the continuous water column in the xylem.
]

#sol-item(21, "B", dense: true)[
  Xylem vessels are dead and hollow, which is why water flows through them freely.
]

#sol-item(22, "A", dense: true)[
  Pure water has the highest water potential; solutes lower it.
]

#sol-item(23, "A", dense: true)[
  Mainly on the lower surface, where they are shaded and lose less water.
]

#sol-item(24, "D", dense: true)[
  Turgid guard cells bow outwards, opening the pore between them.
]

#sol-item(25, "A", dense: true)[
  Transpiration does the first three; glucose is made in photosynthesis.
]

#sol-item(26, "B", dense: true)[
  Cohesion between water molecules holds the column together as it is pulled upwards.
]

#sol-item(27, "D", dense: true)[
  Removing humid air keeps the diffusion gradient steep.
]

#sol-item(28, "D", dense: true)[
  Rolling traps humid air and reduces the exposed surface.
]

#sol-item(29, "D", dense: true)[
  Millions of long, thin extensions give a very large surface area.
]

#sol-item(30, "D", dense: true)[
  With stomata closed and air cooler and damper, water loss falls below uptake and turgor returns.
]

